# Monte Carlo estimate of π

**Method.** Points (x, y) were drawn uniformly in [0, 1)² and counted as inside the quarter circle when x² + y² ≤ 1; the estimate is 4 × inside / total. The study used 20 independent replicates of 1,000,000 points each, from NumPy `PCG64` streams spawned from `SeedSequence(20261019)` (spawn keys 0–19). The 20 replicate estimates were summarised by their arithmetic mean and a two-sided 95% Student t interval across replicates (19 degrees of freedom, t = 2.0930, mean ± t × SD/√20).

**Results.**

| Quantity | Value |
|---|---|
| Mean estimate of π (20 replicates) | **3.141715** (3.1417146) |
| 95% confidence interval | **[3.140917, 3.142512]** (half-width 0.000798) |
| Sample SD across replicates / standard error | 0.001704 / 0.000381 |
| Error against `math.pi` = 3.141593 | **+0.000122** (mean − π); absolute error 0.000122 (0.0039% of π) |
| Points drawn in all | **20,000,000** (20 × 1,000,000); 15,708,573 fell inside |

`math.pi` lies inside the interval, so the estimate is consistent with π. The error is about a third of the standard error (0.32 SEM), as expected for a single run of this size.

**Checks.** The observed across-replicate SD (0.001704) is close to the value expected for a Bernoulli count at this sample size, √(π(4−π)/10⁶) = 0.001642 (variance ratio 1.077, approximate two-sided p = 0.73). With only 20 replicates this check has limited power, and the interval is approximate because it relies on the replicate means being roughly normal. An independent script re-aggregating the saved counts reproduced every figure above. No extra pilot draws were made, and no seed or sample size was chosen after seeing results.

